End-of-session checklist for Claude Code
=========================================

When asked to run this checklist, do the following steps in order:

Note: if you have to stop running the checklist and inform the user of
something, be precise about the issue you are raising. Do not report what
was successful; only say precisely why you stopped running the checklist
(which file, which line, what the problem is, and what you suggest).
If the checklist runs to the end, finish with a summary titled
`Checklist complete!`: the commit hash, the build result, and what each
step changed (files edited, imports removed, ToDo / mind map updates),
skipping steps that changed nothing.

1. Check whether there is anything that has not been committed yet
   (potential non-chores):
       git status --short
   If there are any uncommitted changes (modified, staged or untracked
   files), STOP: do not continue with the checklist, and inform the user,
   listing the files. ONLY continue with the checklist if the user then
   tells you to ignore this and continue.

2. Check every file of the project for warnings, ignoring the
   "declaration uses `sorry`" warnings and the warnings created by
   `#check`s (e.g. the unused tactic linter's "`#check ...` does nothing").
   Build every module explicitly, so that files not yet imported by
   CMAbelianVarieties.lean are checked too (lake also replays the warnings
   of files that are already built):
       lake build $(find CMAbelianVarieties -name '*.lean' | sed 's/\.lean$//; s#/#.#g') \
         2>&1 | grep -E "^(warning|error)" | grep -v "declaration uses .sorry." \
         | grep -v "#check"
   If there are any such warnings (or errors), STOP: do not continue with
   the checklist, and inform the user, listing each warning with its file
   and line. ONLY continue with the checklist if the user then tells you
   to continue.

3. Update the ToDo list (CMAbelianVarieties/ToDo) to reflect the work done
   in this session: remove every task that has been completed (do not just
   mark it as done), and add any new tasks or open problems that came up.
   Only pending tasks should remain in the ToDo list.

4. Go through every file of the project and check that its imports are
   minimal. `#min_imports` is a starting point, but it is not enough on
   its own: some files contain many `#check`s (or examples, tests, etc.)
   whose dependencies it counts too, so read each file and judge which
   imports its actual declarations need. Look for:
     - imports that are not needed at all;
     - imports that are heavier than required (a smaller Mathlib or project
       file would suffice);
     - redundant imports (one import is already implied by another in the
       same file), or an otherwise inefficient list of imports.
   If any file has such a problem, STOP: do not continue with the
   checklist, and inform the user, listing each file with the problematic
   imports and the suggested replacement. From there on, follow the
   user's instructions.

5. Run mk_all to regenerate CMAbelianVarieties.lean so that it imports every
   file in the project:
       lake exe mk_all
   (Test files live in the top-level Test/ folder, outside the library, so
   mk_all does not import them.)

6. Check whether the mind map (MindMap.md) is still up to
   date: compare it with the project's own imports (ignore Mathlib), e.g.:
       grep -rnE "^(public )?import (all )?CMAbelianVarieties" CMAbelianVarieties
   If files were added, removed or renamed, imports changed, or a file's
   purpose changed, update the mind map to match.

7. Build the whole project and check for errors:
       lake build
   Report any errors (and relevant warnings) back to the user.

8. If `lake build` finished with no errors, commit all changes directly to
   the master branch. DO NOT CREATE A NEW BRANCH. Make sure you are on
   master first (`git branch --show-current`), then commit with a chore
   message, e.g.:
       git add -A
       git commit -m "chore: complete end-of-session checklist"
   If there were errors, do NOT commit; report the errors instead.
